WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.101
So far, we've seen how a research question

2
00:00:04.181 --> 00:00:08.101
leads us to a population, then a sample, and
finally

3
00:00:08.181 --> 00:00:12.049
a table that organises all the data we've
collected.

4
00:00:12.129 --> 00:00:14.609
That's a useful start, but with

5
00:00:14.689 --> 00:00:17.756
hundreds or even thousands of observations,

6
00:00:17.836 --> 00:00:20.903
making sense of it all can become a real
headache.

7
00:00:20.983 --> 00:00:22.504
Let's take an example.

8
00:00:22.584 --> 00:00:25.144
Suppose we've recorded

9
00:00:25.236 --> 00:00:27.763
the maximum temperature each day at
Geneva-Cointrin.

10
00:00:27.843 --> 00:00:30.840
We want to examine its distribution.

11
00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:33.656
We have 365 observations. Each row represents

12
00:00:33.736 --> 00:00:36.942
one day of the year and its maximum
temperature.

13
00:00:37.022 --> 00:00:40.905
Which values occur most often?

14
00:00:40.985 --> 00:00:43.773
How widely are they spread?

15
00:00:43.853 --> 00:00:46.200
Are some values rare?

16
00:00:46.292 --> 00:00:48.549
To explore this, we'll build

17
00:00:48.629 --> 00:00:51.372
a histogram, one way of displaying a
distribution.

18
00:00:51.452 --> 00:00:54.293
More generally, a distribution describes

19
00:00:54.373 --> 00:00:58.041
how the values are spread out; a graph helps
us see that.

20
00:00:58.121 --> 00:01:00.816
Here, we have an empirical distribution.

21
00:01:00.896 --> 00:01:02.277
What does 'empirical' mean?

22
00:01:02.357 --> 00:01:04.662
It means based on observations.

23
00:01:04.742 --> 00:01:07.680
We've taken values we actually observed

24
00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:10.747
and grouped them to create this histogram.

25
00:01:10.827 --> 00:01:14.040
We'll look at how to build one in a moment.

26
00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:16.402
So this is an empirical distribution,

27
00:01:16.482 --> 00:01:19.664
based on data we've actually collected.

28
00:01:20.532 --> 00:01:23.096
How do we construct a histogram?

29
00:01:23.176 --> 00:01:26.908
First, we need to talk about bins.

30
00:01:26.988 --> 00:01:30.474
We divide the horizontal axis, here showing

31
00:01:30.554 --> 00:01:35.454
maximum temperature, into intervals from 0 to
40 degrees.

32
00:01:35.534 --> 00:01:38.097
Each interval is 5 degrees wide:

33
00:01:38.177 --> 00:01:41.360
we have bins from 0 to 5, 5 to 10, and so on.

36
00:01:42.031 --> 00:01:45.919
If we have a value of 12.4 degrees,

37
00:01:45.999 --> 00:01:50.108
we put it in the corresponding bin,

38
00:01:50.188 --> 00:01:54.304
between 10 and 15 degrees. A bin is simply an
interval.

39
00:01:54.396 --> 00:01:57.800
We then place each temperature observed at

40
00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:01.720
Geneva-Cointrin into its corresponding bin.

41
00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:04.644
For example, this value of 5 degrees

42
00:02:04.724 --> 00:02:07.817
goes in the bin from 5 to 10 degrees, and so
on.

43
00:02:07.897 --> 00:02:11.115
Every time a value falls into a bin,

44
00:02:11.195 --> 00:02:14.412
we increase that bar's height by one.

45
00:02:14.492 --> 00:02:16.714
That's how we build

46
00:02:16.794 --> 00:02:20.369
this histogram. It shows us that we have more

47
00:02:20.449 --> 00:02:23.697
values between 10 and 15 degrees than, for
example,

48
00:02:23.777 --> 00:02:27.874
between 35 and 40 degrees, which are
relatively rare.

49
00:02:27.954 --> 00:02:33.160
We've now grouped all 365 observations. Adding
up

50
00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:38.592
the heights of the bars must give 365.

51
00:02:39.868 --> 00:02:43.566
Our histogram lets us see several

52
00:02:43.646 --> 00:02:47.758
features of the data's distribution.

53
00:02:47.838 --> 00:02:50.243
First, how often do the values occur?

54
00:02:50.323 --> 00:02:53.710
Let's look again at the bin from 10 to 15
degrees.

55
00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:55.936
We can see that 77 days of the year

56
00:02:56.016 --> 00:02:57.398
fall into this bin.

57
00:02:57.478 --> 00:02:59.591
It doesn't tell us exactly how the values

58
00:02:59.671 --> 00:03:02.358
are distributed within the bin.

59
00:03:02.438 --> 00:03:05.282
I don't know whether more temperatures are
close to 10

60
00:03:05.362 --> 00:03:08.415
or to 15 degrees. But I do know that on 77
days,

61
00:03:08.495 --> 00:03:11.443
the maximum temperature was between 10 and 15
degrees.

62
00:03:11.523 --> 00:03:13.664
That's what the height of the bar tells us.

63
00:03:14.236 --> 00:03:18.384
We can also ask about the range of this
distribution.

64
00:03:18.464 --> 00:03:21.971
The range is simply the difference between

65
00:03:22.051 --> 00:03:26.455
the smallest and largest values in the
dataset.

66
00:03:26.535 --> 00:03:31.580
Looking at the raw data, the values run from 1
to 36.4 degrees.

67
00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:34.270
From the histogram alone, we might

68
00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:37.745
think the values extend from 0 to 40 degrees.

69
00:03:38.222 --> 00:03:39.962
We can also ask about the

70
00:03:40.042 --> 00:03:42.282
overall shape of the distribution.

71
00:03:42.362 --> 00:03:43.693
Is it symmetrical?

72
00:03:43.773 --> 00:03:46.514
Are there more observations on one side than
the other?

73
00:03:46.594 --> 00:03:49.335
Here, for example, we see more relatively

74
00:03:49.415 --> 00:03:52.474
low temperatures than relatively high ones.

75
00:03:52.554 --> 00:03:54.749
The distribution is somewhat asymmetrical.

76
00:03:54.829 --> 00:03:57.798
We can also ask whether there are one or
several groups.

77
00:03:57.878 --> 00:04:00.562
It's hard to tell here, but we can see

78
00:04:00.654 --> 00:04:03.712
one broad peak. We might ask

79
00:04:03.792 --> 00:04:07.048
whether there are distinct subpopulations.

80
00:04:07.128 --> 00:04:10.086
Are there any unusual values?

81
00:04:10.166 --> 00:04:13.075
Here, for example, we have a very high
temperature,

82
00:04:13.155 --> 00:04:16.113
with very few observations in that category.

83
00:04:16.193 --> 00:04:18.752
It might be a measurement error.

84
00:04:18.832 --> 00:04:22.418
If so, we should check it and correct it
before analysing the data.

85
00:04:22.510 --> 00:04:26.443
But it might also be the most interesting part
of the dataset:

86
00:04:26.523 --> 00:04:30.612
a rare event that points us towards something
worth investigating.

87
00:04:30.692 --> 00:04:34.313
We shouldn't automatically assume it's an
error.

88
00:04:34.393 --> 00:04:38.586
We'll return to this point several times.

89
00:04:38.666 --> 00:04:41.140
In any case, we should start by identifying

90
00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:43.826
these surprising values before carrying out
our analysis.

91
00:04:44.590 --> 00:04:48.270
We can now read the table's data visually.

92
00:04:48.350 --> 00:04:50.364
But remember: we lose some precision

93
00:04:50.444 --> 00:04:52.743
compared with examining every observation
individually.

94
00:04:52.823 --> 00:04:54.552
As I mentioned earlier,

95
00:04:54.632 --> 00:04:58.454
we can't tell whether more values are close to
10 degrees

96
00:04:58.534 --> 00:05:00.504
or to 15 degrees. We only know

97
00:05:00.584 --> 00:05:02.870
that many values lie between 10 and 15.

98
00:05:02.950 --> 00:05:05.550
The table therefore retains

99
00:05:05.630 --> 00:05:08.494
the most detailed information.

100
00:05:08.574 --> 00:05:10.492
The histogram is a way

101
00:05:10.572 --> 00:05:13.778
to make the data easier to see.

102
00:05:15.662 --> 00:05:17.798
For a readable histogram,

103
00:05:17.878 --> 00:05:21.207
we also need to choose the bin width
carefully.

104
00:05:21.287 --> 00:05:25.070
Here, bins of 5 degrees provide

105
00:05:25.150 --> 00:05:29.127
a reasonable balance between detail and
readability.

106
00:05:29.607 --> 00:05:32.525
Too few bins

107
00:05:32.605 --> 00:05:35.880
hide details of the distribution.

108
00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:38.929
But too many bins can

109
00:05:39.009 --> 00:05:42.486
make the graph hard to read and obscure the
pattern.

110
00:05:42.566 --> 00:05:44.722
A useful histogram makes it much easier

111
00:05:44.802 --> 00:05:47.416
to see the distribution's overall shape.

112
00:05:47.496 --> 00:05:51.329
So a histogram of an empirical distribution
isn't just

113
00:05:51.409 --> 00:05:55.627
an illustration for a report. It's also an
important tool

114
00:05:55.719 --> 00:05:59.602
in exploratory data analysis. It helps us spot

115
00:05:59.682 --> 00:06:03.675
patterns, asymmetries and unusual values

116
00:06:03.755 --> 00:06:08.079
that can lead to a better understanding of our
dataset.

117
00:06:08.159 --> 00:06:11.877
Now let's focus on summary measures

118
00:06:11.957 --> 00:06:15.755
that help us describe our data quantitatively.

119
00:06:16.007 --> 00:06:19.986
The first is one you all know: the mean.

120
00:06:20.066 --> 00:06:23.071
For example, let's take some journey times.

121
00:06:23.151 --> 00:06:26.751
Here are seven journeys, with their durations
in minutes.

122
00:06:26.831 --> 00:06:29.348
They add up to 280 minutes, and the journey
times

123
00:06:29.428 --> 00:06:32.108
vary quite a lot.

124
00:06:32.188 --> 00:06:33.678
We have values of 34 and 21,

125
00:06:33.758 --> 00:06:35.842
and at the other end, a value of 110.

126
00:06:35.922 --> 00:06:37.791
What does the mean do?

127
00:06:37.871 --> 00:06:41.146
It shares the total equally among all the
observations.

128
00:06:41.226 --> 00:06:43.499
If I take the total of 280 minutes

129
00:06:43.591 --> 00:06:46.653
and divide by seven, I get 40 minutes.

130
00:06:46.733 --> 00:06:50.222
It's as if each journey were assigned a
duration of 40 minutes.

131
00:06:50.302 --> 00:06:52.192
Be careful with the mean, though:

132
00:06:52.272 --> 00:06:54.749
it's quite sensitive to extreme values.

133
00:06:54.829 --> 00:06:57.199
Here, for example, the value of

134
00:06:57.279 --> 00:07:00.075
110 pulls the mean up considerably.

135
00:07:00.155 --> 00:07:03.682
Without it, the mean would fall to 28.3
minutes.

136
00:07:03.762 --> 00:07:07.032
We must be careful about removing an
observation.

137
00:07:07.112 --> 00:07:10.124
As I said earlier, it could be an error,

138
00:07:10.204 --> 00:07:13.886
or it could be interesting information. But
this shows that the mean

139
00:07:13.966 --> 00:07:17.419
isn't always the most useful summary.

140
00:07:18.019 --> 00:07:20.349
Because of that sensitivity, we'll introduce

141
00:07:20.429 --> 00:07:23.168
a second measure: the median.

142
00:07:23.248 --> 00:07:25.834
First, we take all the observations

143
00:07:25.914 --> 00:07:29.115
and arrange them in ascending order.

144
00:07:29.195 --> 00:07:33.217
We sort them from smallest to largest and find
the middle value.

145
00:07:33.297 --> 00:07:36.293
The median splits the ordered data in half:

146
00:07:36.373 --> 00:07:40.115
one half below it and one half above it.

147
00:07:40.207 --> 00:07:43.016
With seven values, there are three on each
side.

148
00:07:43.096 --> 00:07:45.905
The central value, 27, is the median.

149
00:07:45.985 --> 00:07:49.310
Finding the middle depends on the number of
observations.

150
00:07:49.390 --> 00:07:52.973
Here we had an odd number: three on each side
and one in the centre.

151
00:07:53.053 --> 00:07:56.120
With an even number, here six values,

152
00:07:56.200 --> 00:07:59.370
there are three on either side. We take

153
00:07:59.450 --> 00:08:02.803
the mean of the two central values.

154
00:08:02.895 --> 00:08:07.995
That's why the median here is 24 minutes.

155
00:08:08.075 --> 00:08:12.221
Compare how the two measures respond to the
change.

156
00:08:12.301 --> 00:08:16.379
The mean was 40 minutes. Removing the value

157
00:08:16.459 --> 00:08:20.741
of 110 brought it down to 28.3 minutes.

158
00:08:20.821 --> 00:08:23.195
By contrast, removing 110

159
00:08:23.275 --> 00:08:26.547
changed the median from 27 to 24.

160
00:08:26.639 --> 00:08:33.639
In this sense, the median is less sensitive to
extreme values.

161
00:08:34.799 --> 00:08:39.150
There's another measure we can use: the mode.

162
00:08:39.230 --> 00:08:41.033
What is the mode?

163
00:08:41.113 --> 00:08:43.051
It's simply

164
00:08:43.631 --> 00:08:46.482
the most frequent value in the data.

165
00:08:46.562 --> 00:08:50.030
Looking again at these seven journeys, 21
minutes

166
00:08:50.110 --> 00:08:53.681
appears twice, so that's the mode.

167
00:08:53.761 --> 00:08:56.870
There are more values of 21 than of any other
value.

168
00:08:56.950 --> 00:08:59.595
One advantage of the mode is that it isn't

169
00:08:59.675 --> 00:09:03.040
limited to the numerical values we've used

170
00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:06.537
for the mean and median: it also works for
categories.

171
00:09:06.617 --> 00:09:09.651
For example, here are different forms of
transport:

172
00:09:09.743 --> 00:09:12.135
cycling, public transport, walking, and so on.

173
00:09:12.215 --> 00:09:15.788
Cycling is the mode of this series

174
00:09:15.868 --> 00:09:19.522
because it appears 18 times, more often than
any other category.

175
00:09:20.003 --> 00:09:23.342
That's a useful feature of the mode.

176
00:09:23.422 --> 00:09:26.429
It works for both categorical and numerical
variables;

177
00:09:26.509 --> 00:09:29.800
our mean and median examples used numerical
variables.

178
00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:31.604
But the mode also has drawbacks.

179
00:09:31.684 --> 00:09:34.359
One concerns measurement precision.

180
00:09:34.439 --> 00:09:37.398
Temperature, for example, is continuous.

181
00:09:37.478 --> 00:09:39.677
As we saw in the previous video,

182
00:09:39.757 --> 00:09:42.384
'continuous' means that however much we
increase

183
00:09:42.464 --> 00:09:45.328
the precision, there are still possible values
in between.

184
00:09:45.408 --> 00:09:47.687
We aren't restricted to separate counting
steps.

185
00:09:47.907 --> 00:09:52.435
If temperatures are rounded to whole degrees,
we might have

186
00:09:52.515 --> 00:09:57.397
18, 18, 21, 21: here 18 is the mode, appearing
three times overall.

187
00:09:57.477 --> 00:10:00.823
But with greater precision, we find

188
00:10:00.903 --> 00:10:04.663
that those readings of 18 degrees are actually
all different.

189
00:10:04.743 --> 00:10:07.972
A different mode appears: 20.5 degrees.

190
00:10:08.052 --> 00:10:11.043
If we increase the precision again,

191
00:10:11.123 --> 00:10:14.727
there is no longer a unique mode.

192
00:10:14.819 --> 00:10:18.348
As precision increases, it eventually becomes

193
00:10:18.428 --> 00:10:22.254
very unlikely that two measurements will be
exactly equal.

194
00:10:22.334 --> 00:10:26.012
So the mode isn't always intuitive

195
00:10:26.092 --> 00:10:30.047
for continuous variables. Keep that limitation
in mind.

196
00:10:30.595 --> 00:10:33.432
How do we choose the right measure in
practice?

197
00:10:33.512 --> 00:10:35.572
There's no universal recipe.

198
00:10:35.652 --> 00:10:38.489
We need to understand what each measure
describes

199
00:10:38.569 --> 00:10:41.504
so we can use it in an appropriate setting.

200
00:10:41.584 --> 00:10:43.886
The mean gives us a sense of the overall
level,

201
00:10:43.966 --> 00:10:46.366
sharing the total equally across observations.

202
00:10:46.446 --> 00:10:48.262
The median identifies the centre

203
00:10:48.342 --> 00:10:50.919
of an ordered series.

204
00:10:51.011 --> 00:10:54.695
The mode identifies the most common value.
Always consider

205
00:10:54.775 --> 00:10:58.724
your research question when choosing a summary
measure.

206
00:10:58.804 --> 00:11:01.534
As we've seen, the median

207
00:11:01.614 --> 00:11:04.449
is less sensitive to extreme values.

208
00:11:04.529 --> 00:11:07.948
Our example had unusually high values,

209
00:11:08.028 --> 00:11:11.341
but unusually low ones can also matter.

210
00:11:11.421 --> 00:11:14.946
The mean is more sensitive, while the mode

211
00:11:15.026 --> 00:11:18.631
can depend on measurement precision for a
continuous variable.

212
00:11:20.643 --> 00:11:23.664
Let's take a short detour through some

213
00:11:23.744 --> 00:11:26.963
pitfalls we should avoid.

214
00:11:27.043 --> 00:11:31.384
Suppose I tell you:

215
00:11:31.464 --> 00:11:35.951
'My users' average journey time has increased
by 12%.'

216
00:11:36.451 --> 00:11:39.107
One question we might ask is:

217
00:11:39.187 --> 00:11:42.640
have all the journeys become longer?

218
00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:46.914
Perhaps one or two journeys, or a few more,

219
00:11:46.994 --> 00:11:51.246
have pulled the mean up enough to produce that

220
00:11:51.326 --> 00:11:55.577
12% increase, while most journey times haven't
changed.

221
00:11:55.657 --> 00:11:58.940
Or perhaps all, or most,

222
00:11:59.020 --> 00:12:02.439
of the journeys have become about 12% longer.

223
00:12:03.051 --> 00:12:06.897
So this information is incomplete.

224
00:12:06.977 --> 00:12:11.314
The figure of 12% doesn't tell us the change
in absolute terms either.

225
00:12:11.394 --> 00:12:14.258
If the initial mean was 10 minutes,

226
00:12:14.338 --> 00:12:18.130
a 12% increase adds just 1.2 minutes.

227
00:12:18.210 --> 00:12:21.565
But if the initial mean was 50 minutes,

228
00:12:21.645 --> 00:12:25.135
a 12% increase means an extra six minutes.

229
00:12:25.227 --> 00:12:28.575
So the same relative change of 12%

230
00:12:28.655 --> 00:12:32.285
can represent quite different changes in
actual journey times.

231
00:12:32.365 --> 00:12:35.657
A headline saying 'Average journey time
increased by 12%'

232
00:12:35.737 --> 00:12:38.355
isn't enough to give us the full picture,

233
00:12:38.435 --> 00:12:42.570
because the starting values are missing.

234
00:12:42.650 --> 00:12:46.191
Be careful when interpreting figures like
this.

235
00:12:46.935 --> 00:12:48.790
Now let's turn to another distinction:

236
00:12:48.870 --> 00:12:51.692
observations versus a theoretical model.

237
00:12:51.772 --> 00:12:54.492
Earlier, we used a histogram

238
00:12:54.572 --> 00:12:57.444
to display the empirical distribution in bins.

239
00:12:57.524 --> 00:12:59.226
But a distribution isn't necessarily

240
00:12:59.306 --> 00:13:01.772
empirical: it can also be theoretical.

241
00:13:01.852 --> 00:13:05.161
We'll encounter a few theoretical
distributions in this course.

242
00:13:05.661 --> 00:13:08.968
What does a theoretical distribution
represent?

243
00:13:09.048 --> 00:13:11.750
It describes a possible pattern of values

244
00:13:11.830 --> 00:13:14.592
using a mathematical model.

245
00:13:14.672 --> 00:13:18.160
Here we'll look at the normal,

246
00:13:18.240 --> 00:13:22.635
or Gaussian, distribution, one of the most
widely used.

247
00:13:22.715 --> 00:13:24.389
What do we have here?

248
00:13:24.469 --> 00:13:26.465
The horizontal axis

249
00:13:26.557 --> 00:13:30.674
shows the possible values; the vertical axis
shows density.

250
00:13:30.754 --> 00:13:35.362
Density indicates where values are relatively
more concentrated.

251
00:13:35.442 --> 00:13:38.633
The Gaussian distribution has a mean,

252
00:13:38.713 --> 00:13:41.958
which in this case is also the median,

253
00:13:42.038 --> 00:13:45.447
because a Gaussian, or normal, distribution is
always

254
00:13:45.527 --> 00:13:48.961
symmetrical. Half of its probability lies to
the left

255
00:13:49.053 --> 00:13:51.446
and half to the right of the mean,

256
00:13:51.526 --> 00:13:55.001
so the mean and median coincide.

257
00:13:55.741 --> 00:13:59.315
Here is the formula for the normal density.
You don't need

258
00:13:59.395 --> 00:14:02.876
to memorise it, but it's worth looking at its
structure

259
00:14:02.956 --> 00:14:06.858
to understand how the distribution works.

260
00:14:06.938 --> 00:14:09.528
It is characterised by two parameters:

261
00:14:09.608 --> 00:14:13.182
the mean, represented by

262
00:14:13.262 --> 00:14:17.057
the Greek letter mu, and the standard
deviation, sigma.

263
00:14:17.149 --> 00:14:20.059
The latter describes the spread of the
distribution

264
00:14:20.139 --> 00:14:23.389
and helps determine how often distant values
occur.

265
00:14:23.857 --> 00:14:27.716
Again, you don't need to memorise this
formula.

266
00:14:27.796 --> 00:14:31.857
Let's instead see what happens when we change
the two parameters.

267
00:14:31.937 --> 00:14:35.139
Here, the mean is 5 and the standard deviation
is 1.

268
00:14:35.219 --> 00:14:37.664
Changing the mean simply

269
00:14:37.744 --> 00:14:40.492
shifts the curve left or right.

270
00:14:40.572 --> 00:14:44.309
With a mean of 3, it shifts to the left.

271
00:14:44.401 --> 00:14:47.355
With a mean of 7, it shifts to the right.

272
00:14:47.435 --> 00:14:49.603
So the mean determines the curve's position.

273
00:14:49.683 --> 00:14:53.255
The standard deviation changes its spread.

274
00:14:53.335 --> 00:14:56.852
If we reduce the standard deviation to 0.6,

275
00:14:56.932 --> 00:15:00.617
the curve becomes more sharply concentrated
around the mean.

276
00:15:00.697 --> 00:15:03.538
If we increase it to 1.5,

277
00:15:03.618 --> 00:15:06.709
the distribution becomes more spread out.

278
00:15:06.801 --> 00:15:10.144
These two parameters are enough

279
00:15:10.224 --> 00:15:13.316
to describe this theoretical distribution
completely.

280
00:15:13.396 --> 00:15:17.192
As we'll see, it will be useful in many
settings.

281
00:15:17.272 --> 00:15:20.213
Also remember that the total area under the
curve

282
00:15:20.293 --> 00:15:23.283
is always 1. As we said when discussing
probabilities,

283
00:15:23.363 --> 00:15:26.837
the total probability must equal 1.

284
00:15:27.409 --> 00:15:29.211
Now we can ask

285
00:15:29.291 --> 00:15:31.615
how to use this theoretical distribution.

286
00:15:31.695 --> 00:15:34.438
We might initially think:

287
00:15:34.518 --> 00:15:37.469
'I'll find the probability that my value is
exactly 4.'

288
00:15:37.549 --> 00:15:40.397
But that isn't a useful approach for the
normal distribution,

289
00:15:40.477 --> 00:15:43.951
because it's a continuous theoretical
distribution.

290
00:15:44.031 --> 00:15:47.477
Exactly 4 doesn't just mean a rounded value of
4:

291
00:15:47.557 --> 00:15:51.109
it means 4.0000, with infinitely many zeros.

292
00:15:51.189 --> 00:15:54.582
A real measurement doesn't have that infinite
precision.

293
00:15:54.662 --> 00:15:57.653
Under this continuous model, the probability

294
00:15:57.745 --> 00:16:00.391
of any one exact value is zero.

295
00:16:00.471 --> 00:16:03.972
For continuous distributions, we therefore

296
00:16:04.052 --> 00:16:07.554
look at the probability of falling within an
interval.

297
00:16:07.634 --> 00:16:11.937
We can calculate the probability of being
between 4 and 5.

298
00:16:12.017 --> 00:16:15.304
Without going into the details, we integrate
the density

299
00:16:15.384 --> 00:16:18.805
over that interval. Here, this gives about a
34% chance

300
00:16:18.897 --> 00:16:22.281
that x lies between 4 and 5.

301
00:16:22.361 --> 00:16:24.607
That's how we use these distributions.

302
00:16:24.687 --> 00:16:27.477
We won't go into the mathematical details

303
00:16:27.557 --> 00:16:30.693
in this course. Just remember that with a

304
00:16:30.773 --> 00:16:33.761
continuous theoretical distribution, we
consider

305
00:16:33.841 --> 00:16:36.909
intervals of values rather than individual
exact values.

306
00:16:37.393 --> 00:16:40.575
Why is the normal distribution so important,

307
00:16:40.655 --> 00:16:43.894
and why does it appear in so many

308
00:16:43.974 --> 00:16:47.622
physical and ecological systems?

309
00:16:47.702 --> 00:16:51.757
It can arise when many

310
00:16:51.837 --> 00:16:56.358
small effects or errors combine,

311
00:16:56.438 --> 00:17:00.981
under suitable conditions, to produce an
approximately normal pattern.

312
00:17:01.073 --> 00:17:04.422
Suppose we're carrying out a study

313
00:17:04.502 --> 00:17:08.590
that measures concentrations. A sensor might
give

314
00:17:08.670 --> 00:17:12.494
slightly higher readings, while temperature
might

315
00:17:12.574 --> 00:17:16.240
shift readings slightly lower than the true
value.

316
00:17:16.320 --> 00:17:19.722
Many small factors like these can make

317
00:17:19.802 --> 00:17:24.181
measured concentrations fluctuate around their
true values.

318
00:17:24.839 --> 00:17:28.229
These factors can affect a concentration
measurement,

319
00:17:28.309 --> 00:17:32.075
introducing small errors that add together.

320
00:17:32.155 --> 00:17:35.827
In this illustration, opposing effects can
partly offset:

321
00:17:35.907 --> 00:17:39.860
temperature might lower the reading a little,

322
00:17:39.940 --> 00:17:43.659
while the sensor's calibration might push it
slightly higher.

323
00:17:43.739 --> 00:17:47.102
If those effects balance when combined,

324
00:17:47.182 --> 00:17:51.085
we can obtain a roughly symmetrical pattern.

325
00:17:51.165 --> 00:17:53.052
There are then as many errors pushing

326
00:17:53.132 --> 00:17:55.167
upwards as there are pushing downwards.

327
00:17:55.247 --> 00:17:57.085
This is why the normal distribution

328
00:17:57.165 --> 00:17:59.544
appears so often in natural systems.

329
00:17:59.624 --> 00:18:01.739
You'll encounter it in many different
settings.

330
00:18:01.831 --> 00:18:04.414
That doesn't mean all distributions are
normal,

331
00:18:04.494 --> 00:18:08.515
but it is a common pattern in nature.

332
00:18:09.031 --> 00:18:11.568
Some events and phenomena,

333
00:18:11.648 --> 00:18:14.866
however, cannot be described appropriately

334
00:18:14.946 --> 00:18:18.007
by a normal distribution. Take rainfall.

335
00:18:18.087 --> 00:18:20.782
Here we have daily rainfall at

336
00:18:20.862 --> 00:18:23.922
Geneva-Cointrin from 2023 to 2025: 1,096 days.

337
00:18:24.002 --> 00:18:26.435
Very clearly, many daily totals

338
00:18:26.515 --> 00:18:30.413
are small, between 0 and 5 mm,

339
00:18:30.493 --> 00:18:33.739
while very high values are extremely rare.

340
00:18:34.563 --> 00:18:36.962
In fact, there were 651 days

341
00:18:37.042 --> 00:18:40.287
with no measured rainfall at all.

342
00:18:40.367 --> 00:18:44.458
That's why the 0-to-5 mm bin is so tall.

343
00:18:44.538 --> 00:18:47.662
A normal distribution is clearly

344
00:18:47.742 --> 00:18:51.047
unsuitable for this kind of data.

345
00:18:51.127 --> 00:18:55.158
Zooming in reveals high values that were hard
to see,

346
00:18:55.238 --> 00:18:59.591
reaching 47.6 mm in a single day.

347
00:18:59.683 --> 00:19:02.079
With asymmetry like this,

348
00:19:02.159 --> 00:19:04.554
a normal distribution is not appropriate.

349
00:19:04.634 --> 00:19:07.470
Another situation where a single normal
distribution

350
00:19:07.550 --> 00:19:11.211
can be unsuitable is when distinct subgroups
are present.

351
00:19:11.291 --> 00:19:13.632
This histogram shows

352
00:19:13.712 --> 00:19:16.328
measured survival rates for two species.

353
00:19:16.408 --> 00:19:20.039
The survival rates range from 20% to 95%.

354
00:19:20.131 --> 00:19:22.324
Species A and B clearly

355
00:19:22.404 --> 00:19:25.136
have different distributions.

356
00:19:25.216 --> 00:19:29.323
For species A, there is a peak around 40%
survival;

357
00:19:29.403 --> 00:19:33.929
for species B, another peak appears around
80%.

358
00:19:34.009 --> 00:19:38.056
Fitting a single theoretical normal
distribution here

359
00:19:38.136 --> 00:19:42.503
would hide those two subgroups.

360
00:19:42.595 --> 00:19:46.506
We should examine each species separately

361
00:19:46.586 --> 00:19:50.550
and consider whether a normal model is
appropriate for each.

362
00:19:50.630 --> 00:19:54.864
Species A alone looks fairly

363
00:19:54.944 --> 00:19:59.017
symmetrical and normal, as does species B.

364
00:19:59.097 --> 00:20:03.303
But a single normal distribution doesn't fit
the combined data.

365
00:20:03.395 --> 00:20:07.288
Let's recap. A histogram shows

366
00:20:07.368 --> 00:20:12.211
how a variable's values are distributed:

367
00:20:12.291 --> 00:20:16.540
which values occur and how widely they're
spread.

368
00:20:16.620 --> 00:20:21.107
We also introduced measures that summarise
features of the data:

369
00:20:21.187 --> 00:20:23.559
the median, the mean and the mode.

370
00:20:23.651 --> 00:20:26.568
Then we looked at the normal distribution,

371
00:20:26.648 --> 00:20:29.514
a theoretical distribution useful for many

372
00:20:29.594 --> 00:20:32.861
natural phenomena, whose mean and median
coincide.

373
00:20:32.941 --> 00:20:34.808
The other parameter is standard deviation,

374
00:20:34.888 --> 00:20:37.605
which we'll examine in more detail shortly.

375
00:20:37.685 --> 00:20:41.251
With these two parameters, the normal
distribution

376
00:20:41.331 --> 00:20:45.127
provides a quantitative model for many
phenomena.

377
00:20:45.631 --> 00:20:47.936
We still need a clear description of the

378
00:20:48.016 --> 00:20:50.997
standard deviation. That's the topic of the
next video.
